Z||000000||Wade87GT||09-14-2001||06:05 PM||||I wanted to order an a/c accumulator from gmpartsdirect.com, so I called TWO Pontiac parts depts. and both gave me #2724666. Got the part at about half the price but it's the wrong one! Same part number as mentioned, but it doesn't have the pressure cycling switch  that I need (where the two wire connector attaches onto the accumulator itself). Does anybody here have the correct part number? I've searched here at the Forum already and I'm still looking hard for a P22 parts book also.<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-14-2001||07:10 PM||||The switch is a seperate part. It screws on to one of the Schrader ports. Make sure you remove the valve core from the port you use.<P>If you can't get the existing one of then buy another. Good idea to buy another anyway. That switch wears. no sense doing all that work to have the switch crap out next month.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wade87GT||09-14-2001||09:06 PM||||Thanks theogre, you're right about the Schrader port hook up. But upon closer inspection I see that my new accumulator has its Schrader ports mounted 180 degrees from each other (12:00 + 6:00 positions). The rear Schrader on the old one is mounted on the flat spot just aft of the left hose connector, or 8:00. When viewing the old accumulator from the top, when installed, call the front Schrader the 12:00 position, left hose 9:00, rear Schrader 8:00, right hose 1:00. My concern is that the pressure switch, with its long length, will be rubbing on the bulkhead. This A/C stuff is too expensive to be doing any rigging. Also, my neighbor is a parts store guy and said, "Yep, they're different."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-15-2001||12:55 AM||||Yet another reason I like the V5.... no freaking clutch cycle switch. Too bad it'd be a major _____ to change it from the A6 family.<P>You only option seems to be, return the GM one and go third party.||||reg||1
